ting the information from someone  else I'm not exactly sure why he did  that I think he thought if he really  told us he was a VC we'd never believe  him because we'd had so many that that  just never were but it turned out this  guy actually was a VC but at the time of  the evacuation we offered to get him out  and he refused that gave me the idea  well maybe he wasn't really maybe he was  working actually working for the VC and  they were using him as a conduit to get  information that they wanted us to have  for whatever you know as convoluted as  that may seem as it turns out the guy  was the VC he was working for us the  military several other organizations  a real paper mill he gave us absolutely  great information but they arrested his  case officer the day that Vietnam fell  and he blew the whistle on this guy and  the agent fireball committed suicide  before we before he could be arrested  that is the only if you will penetration  that we had of the VC infrastructure  that I actually know was a VC but at the  time we did not think we did not know he  was actually a VC excuse me can you tell  me how accurate the polygraph is there  is okay this is what makes it art and  not science on again on a particular  situation Alfred recently I was called  by channel 7 and asked if I thought they  should test the people out at Fort  Detrick regarding this anthrax and I  said that's what the polygraph was made  for one issue did you take it or any way  involved in the removal of that anthrax  from the facility I said there are two  problems here one the person who took it  may not work there anymore you don't  know whether that person is still there  I absolutely believe if the person who  took it were to be polygraphed it would  show up on the polygraph the problem we  have with polygraph today and  particularly the intelligence we diluted  the more questions we ask the less  reliable it is now for example if one of  you right here now had your wallet  stolen just say you're sitting here also  and you realize your wallet was stolen  if it's a given that someone in this  room had to have taken it I feel quite  confident I could find out who took it I  I do but the more questions you asked  the less reliable it is there are so  many extraneous factors that impact on  the reliability of polygraph the  reaction between the examiner and the  subject can have a significant effect on  polygraph  I always tell junior examiner's or  people who came in after all after I did  that the person who tests you does not  have to like you but they cannot dislike  you and if they do you will never get an  admission  the most valid polygraph test is one in  which you see a reaction to a question  you advise the person that he's reacted  to the question and you get an  explanation for that reaction that can  be verified polygraph kane' less than  that it's what we call a swag that's a  scientific wild ass guess because you  really don't know that's the best I can  say I have done tests where I felt in  this particular case it was 90% accurate  but until you get that admission you  really don't know but I do think every  circumstances for every circumstance I  think the reliability changes that's as  good an answer as I can give you please  could you add something on the  linguistic dimension of conducting  polygraph exams in another language oh  absolutely  with a good interpreter I think it works  now there is nothing better than being  able to communicate one on one I've had  examples where you know the person I'm  testing will not speak to another for  example if the interpreters from his  country he said you know you'll be going  next year this guy is going to be here  forever and if the politics change he's  going to know that I worked with you  guys so that is a problem I found that  with a good interpreter it's not as  reliable as I would like but with a good  interpreter I think I think it is  effective but I have caught interpreters  I don